AB  - This study proposes a technique for improving identity verification at checkpoint units utilizing cloud-based face recognition technology, which involves a tablet PC with a webcam and a network as a checkpoint station. The system captures video images using WebRTC, while OpenCV-JS is a version specifically designed to function on web browsers that support WASM. The HAAR Cascade Classifier will be used to detect faces inside preprocessed images and post them to the server side through an API. The API will also recognize faces using Amazon Rekognition, resulting in a face ID to look for participant information from the MariaDB database. Participants authenticate themselves and log in; a message is sent to other relevant systems over MQTT. Before accessing the system, user permissions will be granted by integrating with OAuth 2.0 to preserve security. Data on attendance events is gathered and visualized in Power BI dashboards. 126,183 samples, from 2019 to 2024, were used for system evaluation. The system has been utilized for several purposes, including verifying attendance at graduation ceremonies, requesting rights at the COVID-19 vaccination station, and verifying exam admission. The experiment revealed that the new approach could reduce the 33-second waiting time at the checkpoint to only 3 seconds and, at the longest, no more than 7 seconds per attendee. The result is equivalent to a 91% gain in efficiency.
